¶
{{smaller block|Blessed art thou ''among'' women. {{right|Luke i. 28.}}}}
{{smaller block|The years during which Bacon held the Great Seal were ''among'' . . . the most shameful in English history. Everything at home and abroad was mismanaged.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Lord Bacon.}}}}
'''3.''' By the joint action or consent of; with the common aid or knowledge of: as, settle it ''among'' yourselves; the mischief was done ''among'' you.
{{smaller block|You have, ''among'' you, killed a sweet and innocent lady.
{{right|''Shak.'', Much Ado, v. i.}}}}
'''4.''' To each of; by or for distribution to: as, he gave five dollars to be divided ''among'' them.
{{smaller block|What are they [five loaves and two fishes] ''among'' so many? {{right|John vi. 9.}}}}
'''5†.''' In the circumstances of; during the time or term of; in the course of.
{{smaller block|I never went to any place ''among'' all my life . . . which I had before . . . thought of.
{{right|''Baxter'', in Tulloch’s Eng. Puritanism, p. 306. (''N. E. D.'')}}}}
'''II.†''' ''adv.'' '''1.''' Together (with something).
{{smaller block|<poem>Doȝttur, temper well thi tonge,
& vse not monny tallis [many tales];
For lessynggis [leasings] wyll lepe out ''amonge'',
That oftyn brewis ballys [bales, mischiefs].</poem>
{{right|''The Good Wyfe wold a Pylgremage'', l. 85. (E. E. T. S., [extra ser. VIII., i. 41.)}}}}
'''2.''' At intervals; here and there.
{{smaller block|<poem>They [the fowles] sate ''amonge''
Upon my chambre roofe withoute,
Upon the tyles over al aboute.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Death of Blanche, l. 298.}}}}
